      <h1>Energy &amp; infrastructure</h1>
      <p class="sl-contract">The figures are sourced. The argument and forecasts are mine.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-stamp">Q3 2026 · Published Jul 24, 2026 · Data through Jun 30, 2026</div>
</section>
  <p class="sl-lede">A company that decides to build a factory in Mexico needs three things at the exact address it picked: an electricity connection with a date it can plan around, water in a country where industry has concentrated in the driest states, and a way to move containers to a port or the border. Two state companies control most of that. Pemex, the state oil company, sells the fuel, and CFE, the state electricity utility, owns the wires, and keeping both solvent costs the federal budget money it also needs for grids and water systems.</p>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="sec-head"><h2>How it works</h2><div class="sec-note">Last revised Aug 1, 2026</div></div>
    <p class="p">Start with Pemex. Crude production has fallen for two decades, the refineries lose money, and the debt is large enough that the federal government has repeatedly stepped in with cash injections and tax relief. The loop is the point: the budget still counts on oil income, and that income comes from a company the budget keeps rescuing. Pemex is not financially separate from the state the way a private company would be, because federal support, tax breaks and government-backed refinancing all draw on the same public capacity, and Moody's holds Mexico on the last investment-grade rung chiefly because of what that support costs. Pemex reported a smaller debt load at the end of 2025 than a year earlier, in its own annual filing, which is real progress on one line of the accounts. The open question is whether its operations can fund investment and pay suppliers without another transfer.</p>
    <p class="p">Fuel at the pump is a managed price rather than a free one. The government smooths it by adjusting the IEPS, the federal tax on fuel, and through periodic price agreements with retailers, so the number on the sign moves far less than crude does. A fuel-tax discount is revenue the budget does not collect, which is money that is not building transmission lines.</p>
    <p class="p">CFE owns the wires. A 2021 reform gave CFE's own plants first call on the grid ahead of private generators, which left private generation legal but worth less, since a private plant can be ordered to stand down while a state plant runs. For a factory, the question is almost never whether electricity exists somewhere in Mexico. It is whether the power can reach that specific address, and when the interconnection, the permission and the physical link that let a site plug into the grid, is actually granted. Mexico also imports large volumes of the natural gas its power plants burn from the United States, so a fight over cross-border trade reaches the electricity bill too.</p>
    <p class="p">Water is the constraint that gets budgeted last. Industry has concentrated in the north, which is also the driest part of the country, so a site that clears the power question can still fail the water one. Drought in the northern basins has already pushed industrial parks and municipalities into recycling and rationing. A national water-availability figure will not tell you whether a given plant can run, because the supply that binds it is its own basin, where households and farms are drawing on the same water.</p>
    <p class="p">Freight moves on two private rail concessions and a short list of ports: Manzanillo and Lázaro Cárdenas on the Pacific, Veracruz and Altamira on the Gulf. The government is building lines of its own as well, the Tren Maya through the south-east and the Interoceanic Corridor across the narrow strip of land between the Pacific and the Gulf. Those were chosen for regional development as much as for cargo, so the honest measure of them is tonnage actually carried rather than kilometres of track opened.</p>
    <p class="p">Both companies land on the same budget. Mexico's public debt has risen in each of the last two years and Hacienda, the finance ministry, projects a further rise this year, to a level that is still moderate for an economy this size. The debt ratio is not the number that binds. Pensions, interest payments and support for Pemex are growing claims on a small tax take, and they compete directly with the electricity, water and security spending that decides whether an announced factory opens on schedule. Hacienda publishes the debt path and the spending plan together every September in the budget package, which is where that competition becomes legible. That is why the evidence this page follows is physical: interconnections granted, tenders awarded, machinery imported and tonnage actually moved.</p>
  </section>

