  <section class="sec">
    <h2>My view</h2>
    <p class="p">Companies keep announcing plans for Mexico, and then some of them wait: for a grid connection, for a water permit in the dry north, or for space on a rail line. My read is that this waiting, more than the cost of money, is now the tighter limit on Mexican growth. The headline usually quoted against that view is foreign direct investment, which set a record in 2025. Secretaría de Economía's own breakdown in the RNIE registry splits that headline into new money arriving from outside and profits that companies already operating in Mexico chose to keep here, and the reinvested profits are the larger part by a wide margin. So the record is mostly a measure of existing plants doing well, and existing plants already have their electricity and their water permits. A new one has to join the queue for both.</p>
    <p class="p">I read Pemex mainly as a fiscal story rather than an energy one. What it produces matters less to the rest of the economy than what it costs to keep solvent, because that cost competes directly with the transmission lines and water systems the industrial build-out needs. For the coming year I expect more new capacity to be announced by private operators than by the state, and the gap between what is announced and what is connected to stay wide. Anyone weighing the purchase of a Mexican manufacturer will find that the questions setting that plant's value are local rather than national: whether the site holds a signed interconnection with a date on it, which basin its water comes from and whether that basin is under restriction, and how much of its power bill traces back to natural gas imported from the United States. The one national figure worth reading beside those is the new-investment share of foreign direct investment, published quarterly by Secretaría de Economía in the RNIE, the official registry of foreign direct investment.</p>
    <p class="p change-mind"><b>What would change my mind:</b> interconnection approvals and transmission capacity rising fast enough that industrial projects stop naming power and water as the thing they are waiting on. The releases that would show it are CENACE's grid data and CFE's quarterly results, plus the new-investment share in the quarterly RNIE figures. If that happens, the binding question moves from whether power arrives to what it costs.</p>
  </section>

